# Deep Copy of Nested Settings

**JavaScript (using structuredClone or lodash):**

```javascript
const original = {
  api: { url: 'https://api.example.com', timeout: 5000 },
  cache: { ttl: 3600, enabled: true }
};

// Native deep copy (modern JS)
const copy = structuredClone(original);

// Edit the copy
copy.api.timeout = 10000;
copy.cache.enabled = false;

console.log(original.api.timeout); // 5000 (unchanged)
console.log(copy.api.timeout);     // 10000
```

**JavaScript (manual recursive approach if structuredClone unavailable):**

```javascript
function deepCopy(obj) {
  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;
  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(deepCopy);
  return Object.keys(obj).reduce((acc, key) => {
    acc[key] = deepCopy(obj[key]);
    return acc;
  }, {});
}

const copy = deepCopy(original);
```

**Python:**

```python
import copy

original = {
    'api': {'url': 'https://api.example.com', 'timeout': 5000},
    'cache': {'ttl': 3600, 'enabled': True}
}

# Deep copy
settings = copy.deepcopy(original)

# Edit the copy
settings['api']['timeout'] = 10000
settings['cache']['enabled'] = False

print(original['api']['timeout'])  # 5000 (unchanged)
print(settings['api']['timeout'])  # 10000
```

**Why this matters:** A shallow copy (`{...original}` or `Object.assign()`) only copies the top level—nested objects remain references to the original, so mutations cascade up.
